# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — all environments
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate with: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(50))"
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=generate-with-python-secrets-module

# Optional: a dedicated signing key for JWT auth tokens. Defaults to
# DJANGO_SECRET_KEY when unset. Set it (same generator) to rotate the JWT key
# independently of SECRET_KEY (which also signs sessions, CSRF, and other tokens).
# JWT_SIGNING_KEY=generate-with-python-secrets-module

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT
# `python manage.py runserver` uses config.settings.development by default:
#   - SQLite database (no setup needed, db.sqlite3 is created automatically)
#   - Console email backend (magic-link emails are printed to the terminal)
# The values below already work out of the box for local development.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DJANGO_DEBUG=True
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000
CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000
MAGIC_LINK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/verify
SHARE_LINK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/share

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMAGE UPLOADS — Cloudinary (all environments, including local)
# Images upload straight from the browser to Cloudinary; Django only signs
# the request. Without this the app runs fine, but image uploads will fail.
# Create a free account at https://cloudinary.com, then copy the value of
# "API environment variable" from your dashboard (Settings -> API Keys).
# Format: cloudinary://<api_key>:<api_secret>@<cloud_name>
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLOUDINARY_URL=cloudinary://your_api_key:your_api_secret@your_cloud_name

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRODUCTION (self-hosted: Heroku, DigitalOcean, AWS, ...)
# Switch settings to production and replace the local values above with
# your real domain. See HEROKU.md for a full deployment walk-through.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=config.settings.production
# DJANGO_DEBUG=False
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=YOUR-DOMAIN.com
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://YOUR-DOMAIN.com
# CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://YOUR-DOMAIN.com
# MAGIC_LINK_BASE_URL=https://YOUR-DOMAIN.com/verify
# RSVP_BASE_URL=https://YOUR-DOMAIN.com/rsvp
# SHARE_LINK_BASE_URL=https://YOUR-DOMAIN.com/share

# Email — magic-link auth REQUIRES SMTP in production (any provider works,
# e.g. Mailgun, SendGrid). In local development email goes to the console.
# EMAIL_HOST=smtp.mailgun.org
# EMAIL_PORT=587
# EMAIL_HOST_USER=postmaster@YOUR-DOMAIN.com
# EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD=your-smtp-password
# DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL=noreply@YOUR-DOMAIN.com
# Optional: SMTP socket timeout in seconds (default 10). Caps how long a slow or
# hung mail provider can stall a web request before the platform router times out.
# EMAIL_TIMEOUT=10

# Optional: where the /contact support form is delivered. Defaults to
# DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL — i.e. the operator mails themselves.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=hello@YOUR-DOMAIN.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ABUSE GUARDS — all OFF by default, and that is a real decision
# These protect *your* deployment's sending reputation, storage bill and
# moderation load, so only you know the right numbers — the standalone ships
# them unset. Unset or 0 means "no limit" in every case below. Read this
# section before going public: with all of it off, one free pop-in account can
# send unbounded invitation emails from your domain and fill a collection
# without ever meeting a ceiling. See README.md and HEROKU.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Invitation EMAILS one account may send per day (single + bulk combined, so a
# 100-row CSV can't multiply past it). 150 is what www.oiueei.com uses.
# INVITE_EMAILS_PER_DAY=150

# Per-collection mass-upload guards. Each _ALARM quietly emails the superusers
# ONCE when the count is crossed and changes nothing — a tripwire, not a
# warning: the owner is never told, so a real bulk import isn't interrupted and
# someone probing doesn't learn where the line sits. Each _BLOCK refuses adds
# that would cross it (400), checked against whole batches. A superuser lifts
# both ceilings for one collection with `capacity_unblocked` in the admin.
# COLLECTION_THINGS_ALARM=500
# COLLECTION_THINGS_BLOCK=1000
# COLLECTION_INVITES_ALARM=250
# COLLECTION_INVITES_BLOCK=500

# Extra URL modules this deployment mounts alongside OIUEEI's own routes —
# comma-separated dotted paths to Django URLconfs. Unset in the standalone:
# everything the product serves already lives in core.urls.
#
# It is here so a deployment can add views of its own without editing
# config/urls.py, which is the file that would otherwise need patching again
# after every update. Each module mounts at the root and BEFORE the SPA
# catch-all, so a page outside the static/, api/ and admin prefixes actually
# answers instead of being served index.html with a 200.
# DEPLOYMENT_URLCONFS=operator.urls,intranet.urls

# Who may create which kind of collection, and offer a thing under which verb —
# the dotted path to a CreatorPolicy subclass. The default says yes to everyone,
# always, which is OIUEEI as a product: an account is the only requirement.
#
# Point it at your own subclass if this deployment is narrower (only the board
# opens COMMUNITY collections, lending needs vetting, ...). One class governs
# both the refusal and the capabilities the frontend reads from /auth/me/, so
# the UI never offers what the API would refuse. See core/services/creator_policy.py.
# CREATOR_POLICY=core.services.creator_policy.OpenCreatorPolicy

# How many proxies in front of the app are trusted to have appended to
# X-Forwarded-For. This decides which entry the per-IP rate limits bucket on,
# counted from the RIGHT — only the tail of that header is written by a proxy;
# everything left of it is the caller's own text.
#
#   1  (default) one trusted proxy: Heroku, Render, Fly, or an nginx/Caddy you
#      run that sets X-Forwarded-For itself. The last entry is the real client.
#   0  NOTHING trusted in front — gunicorn/uvicorn facing the internet, or a
#      reverse proxy that doesn't set the header. SET THIS, or the header is
#      client-supplied and one caller mints a fresh bucket per request: every
#      IP limit above and below (magic links, joining, contact, CSP reports, the
#      admin login) stops holding.
#   2+ a CDN in front of the platform router — skip the CDN's own hops.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=1
